solution:
**38**

Explanation. The chapter does not print this total anywhere, and it cannot be assembled from the chapter alone. What the chapter does supply is:

- glycolysis oxidises one glucose molecule and forms two molecules each of pyruvic acid, ATP, \(\text{NADH}_2\) and water (p13);
- conversion of those two pyruvic acid molecules into acetyl-CoA releases two \(\text{NADH}_2\) and two \(\text{CO}_2\) (p13);
- the tricarboxylic acid cycle (Krebs cycle) completely oxidises the acetyl part of acetyl-CoA and yields \(\text{CO}_2\), \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\), \(\text{NADH}_2\) and \(\text{FADH}_2\) — the chapter gives no counts for these (p13);
- in the electron transfer chain reaction each \(\text{NADH}_2\) gives 3 molecules of ATP and each \(\text{FADH}_2\) gives 2 molecules of ATP, and this reaction is operated in mitochondria only (p14).

38 is the figure expected at this level and it is consistent with the two conversion rates the chapter prints, but the per-turn Krebs cycle yields needed to arrive at it are not stated in this chapter. The chapter's own conclusion is only the qualitative one: "a molecule of glucose is completely oxidized in aerobic respiration and molecules of \(\text{CO}_2\) and \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) are produced along with energy".
